# Milestone: v0.10.2-reactive (W6, activated post W4-merge)
# Covers: TAG_STOP wakes a sleeping strand mid-sleep.
# blocked: tag-stop resume of a sleeping strand not observable across host-driver lines
# matrix-row: temporal-sleep
#
# The pieces this fixture needs all exist (Tag.new + script .stop() + sleep()
# + the v0.11.4-cat-f `## host: step`/`advance-clock` directives), but the
# end-to-end resume is still not observable from a fixture.  Driven through
# the host-driver line by line:
#
#   ## host: run var t = Tag.new("sleeper")        -> <Tag: sleeper>
#   ## host: run Realm.caught = false              -> false
#   ## host: run t: { try { sleep(500ms) } catch (e) { Realm.caught = true } }
#   ## host: step 2000                             -> step: RUNNING (parked)
#   ## host: run t.stop()                           -> nil
#   ## host: step 2000                             -> step: RUNNING
#   ## host: run Realm.caught                        -> false   (NOT true!)
#
# The sleeping body strand parks when its `## host: run` line returns; the
# later t.stop() runs on a fresh loader strand and DOES walk the tag's member
# strands, but the unblocked sleeper never re-runs its catch block to deposit
# Realm.caught = true.  This is the same lone-strand dispatch-loop gap as
# wait_sleep_basic.chk: a strand made runnable while ready_head is otherwise
# empty does not get dispatched, because urbi_step's loop breaks on a NULL
# sched_pick_next before the resumed strand can execute.
#
# Activating needs the runtime change called out in wait_sleep_basic.chk.
# The tag-stop -> sched_strand_unblock unbind path itself (sleep_q removal +
# make_runnable + UEXEC_TAG_STOP resume) is unit-tested directly.
